const BOOK = [{"slug":"dedication","kicker":"Opening","title":"Dedication & Epigraph","body":"<p><em>To everyone who has ever believed God and still found themselves asking questions.</em></p><p><em>To everyone who has faced a season in which the resources were small, the future uncertain, and the way forward difficult to see.</em></p><p><em>And to every believer learning that peace does not come from knowing everything that will happen, but from knowing the God who will be there.</em></p><p><em>This book is for you.</em></p><h2>Epigraph</h2><p><em>“And they that know thy name will put their trust in thee: for thou, LORD, hast not forsaken them that seek thee.”</em></p><p><strong>Psalm 9:10</strong></p><p><em>“My grace is sufficient for thee: for my strength is made perfect in weakness.”</em></p><p><strong>2 Corinthians 12:9</strong></p>"},{"slug":"preface","kicker":"Preface","title":"Preface","body":"<p>There are moments when the greatest problem confronting us is not the problem itself, but what the problem causes us to believe about God.</p><p>A door closes. Money becomes insufficient. A plan fails. An opportunity appears larger than our capacity. A prayer seems unanswered. The future becomes uncertain. And almost without noticing, we begin drawing conclusions.</p><p>Perhaps God is distant. Perhaps He is reluctant. Perhaps He has forgotten. Perhaps there is no way forward.</p><p>What begins as an observation about our circumstances gradually becomes a conclusion about God.</p><p>This book was written to challenge that movement. Its central concern is not primarily how to overcome fear, achieve success, increase resources, or become more courageous. Those things matter, but they are not the foundation.</p><p>The foundation is knowing God.</p><p>The conviction behind these pages is simple: many of the struggles believers experience with trust are ultimately connected to what we believe God is like. We cannot consistently trust a God we do not know.</p><p>If God is powerful but we are uncertain about His goodness, His power will not necessarily give us peace. If God knows everything but we are uncertain about His love, His knowledge may make us uncomfortable rather than secure. If God possesses unlimited resources but we believe He is reluctant to help, His abundance will seem irrelevant to our lives.</p><p>The issue, therefore, is not merely whether God can. The question is: who is the God who can?</p><p>Grace gives us an extraordinary answer.</p><p>Grace is rightly discussed in connection with salvation. We are saved by grace through faith. But grace also reveals something profound about the nature of the God who saves. Grace tells us that God gives. Grace tells us that God takes initiative. Grace tells us that His goodness does not begin with our sufficiency. Grace tells us that human weakness does not create weakness in God. And ultimately, grace leads us to Jesus Christ, in whom the character of the invisible God becomes visible.</p><p>That is why this is not principally a book about grace as a theological concept. It is a book about the God of grace.</p><p>Throughout these chapters, historical and scientific events are used as illustrations. They are not offered as proof of Scripture. Human history cannot establish the character of God. That revelation belongs to God Himself. The illustrations simply help us examine assumptions: that what is visible is everything that is relevant, that the closing of one channel means the exhaustion of supply, that small resources are useless, or that uncertainty makes faithful action impossible.</p><p>The examples have also been intentionally drawn from many parts of the world. A Nigerian reader should not have to leave his own history at the door in order to enter a Christian argument meant for everyone. Neither should a reader in Asia, Europe, the Americas, or elsewhere feel that faith is being explained through the history of only one culture. Grace is not Western, African, Asian, or American. God is God.</p><p>My hope is that by the end of this book you will not simply have accumulated more information about Him. I hope God Himself will have become larger in your consciousness.</p><p>I hope “I do not know what to do” will no longer automatically become “there is nothing that can be done.” I hope “I do not have enough” will no longer automatically mean “God cannot work here.” I hope an uncertain future will cease to feel like a godless future. I hope difficult circumstances will lose their power to rewrite God’s character.</p><p>And I hope peace will begin to arise, not from the promise that nothing difficult will happen, but from the knowledge that difficulty cannot change who God is.</p><p>From that peace, I hope courage will grow: the courage to pray, think, ask for wisdom, begin, use what is already in your hand, receive help, revise a failed plan, walk through an open door, and sometimes remain still when God has not yet shown the next step.</p><p>This is not confidence in ourselves. It is something far more stable.</p><p>It is confidence born from knowing God.</p><p>The journey therefore begins with a question that will remain with us until the final page:</p><p>Who is God?</p><p>Not who have our fears told us He is? Not who have our disappointments made Him appear to be? Not who have imperfect people sometimes represented Him to be?</p><p>Who has God revealed Himself to be?</p><p>Grace begins the answer.</p>"},{"slug":"note-to-reader","kicker":"A Note to the Reader","title":"A Note to the Reader","body":"<p>Read this book slowly. Its purpose is not simply to give you nine chapters to complete. It is to invite you to reconsider the God you have been trusting.</p><p>As you read, you may recognize that an idea you carry about God came less from Scripture than from disappointment, fear, upbringing, religious culture, or painful experience. Do not be afraid of that discovery. Bring every picture of God back to His revelation in Scripture and, above all, to Jesus Christ.</p><p>Do not rush past the biblical passages quoted throughout the book. Return to them. Read the surrounding verses. Consider what they reveal about God before asking what they promise about you.</p><p>The ultimate objective of these pages is not that you become fascinated with grace. It is that grace leads you to become fascinated with God.</p><p>If, at the end, you know Him a little more clearly, trust Him a little more deeply, rest in Him a little more completely, and walk with Him a little more courageously, this book will have accomplished its purpose.</p>"},{"slug":"chapter-1-the-god-of-grace","kicker":"Chapter One","title":"The God of Grace","body":"<p>In 1821, a Yoruba boy later known as Samuel Ajayi Crowther was captured in a slave raid. He was moved from one owner to another and carried toward a future he had not chosen. In 1822, a British anti-slavery naval patrol intercepted the vessel carrying him. He was taken to Sierra Leone, educated, became a Christian minister and linguist, and in 1864 was consecrated the first African Anglican bishop.</p><p>Nothing about the first chapter of that life announced the later one.</p><p>The violence was real. The captivity was real. Yet captivity was not the whole truth about what his life could become.</p><p>There is a difference between saying, “This is what I can see,” and saying, “This is all there is.”</p><p>We cross that line more often than we realize. “I do not have enough” becomes “It cannot be done.” “I do not know what to do” becomes “There is no answer.” “I cannot understand what God is doing” becomes “God is doing nothing.” A fact about us quietly becomes a conclusion about God.</p><p>Perhaps this is one of the greatest obstacles to knowing Him.</p><p>We have never experienced existence from anywhere except the human side of reality. Everything around us has limits. Our knowledge, strength, money and patience all have boundaries. Even the people who love us most can only know so much, carry so much and do so much.</p><p>Then we come to God carrying a lifetime of experience with limited beings, and unless Scripture corrects us, we begin constructing God with human materials.</p><p>We know people who are generous until generosity becomes expensive. We know relationships in which favour rises and falls with performance. We know institutions where achievement is rewarded and failure reduces access. These patterns are so normal that we can carry their logic into our relationship with God without noticing.</p><p>So we may believe in an all-powerful God while imagining a reluctant God. We may confess that He owns everything while wondering whether He wants to help. We may say He knows all things and still behave as if our crisis has caught Him unprepared.</p><p>It is possible to believe that God exists and still know very little about what He is like.</p><p>That matters because the existence of God alone does not produce trust.</p><p>Power is comforting only when I know something about the character of the One who possesses it. An all-powerful being without goodness would be terrifying. An all-knowing being without love would simply know everything that could be used against me. An ever-present being without mercy would mean there is nowhere to hide.</p><p>So Scripture does not merely reveal what God can do. It reveals who the God who can do all things actually is.</p><p>When Moses said in Exodus 33:18, “I beseech thee, shew me thy glory,” the response was remarkable. In Exodus 34, God proclaimed His name and described Himself as “merciful and gracious, longsuffering, and abundant in goodness and truth.”</p><p>Moses asked for glory.</p><p>God revealed character.</p><p>We are easily impressed by divine power: seas divide, storms cease, the sick recover, the dead are raised. Yet power alone does not tell us whether the One possessing it can be trusted.</p><p>The wonder of Scripture is that the Almighty is also good. The One who needs nothing gives. The One upon whom everything depends shows mercy to those who depend completely upon Him. The One who knows everything is patient with people who know very little.</p><p>This is where grace becomes more than a doctrine.</p><p>Grace tells us something about God.</p><p>We often define grace as unmerited favour. That is useful because it reminds us that grace cannot be earned. But if that becomes our entire understanding, we may reduce grace to God’s solution for human sin.</p><p>Grace certainly deals with sin, but the character from which grace flows is older than the problem it solves.</p><p>Before humanity sinned, God was already giving.</p><p>Genesis does not begin with human beings pleading for assistance. It begins with God creating. Light appears. The earth is formed. Vegetation grows. Living creatures fill the earth. Then humanity arrives.</p><p>Adam does not awaken in an empty world and begin negotiating with God for the resources necessary to survive. He awakens inside provision.</p><p>Before man achieves anything, man receives.</p><p>Paul later says in Acts 17 that God is not served by human hands “as though he needed any thing,” because He gives “to all life, and breath, and all things.”</p><p>God does not give because He is deficient. Before creation, He was God. Creation did not complete Him. Humanity did not supply something missing in Him.</p><p>God’s giving begins in God.</p><p>That changes grace completely.</p><p>In the summer of 1940, thousands of Jewish refugees in Lithuania were desperately seeking routes out of Europe. Chiune Sugihara, a Japanese diplomat in Kovno, issued more than two thousand transit visas that helped refugees travel eastward through the Soviet Union and Japan. He did not possess the power to end the war. He acted with the authority available to him, using it to open a way for people who could not open it for themselves.</p><p>A human act of mercy cannot define divine grace, but it can expose something about our instincts. The people receiving those visas did not first become powerful enough to rescue themselves and then ask for help. Help was meaningful precisely because their own options had become painfully small.</p><p>The gospel moves in the same direction, but infinitely deeper.</p><p>“For God so loved the world, that he gave his only begotten Son.”</p><p>God loved.</p><p>God gave.</p><p><em>Romans 5:8 says that “while we were yet sinners, Christ died for us.”</em></p><p>Notice the order. God’s action came before human improvement.</p><p>John makes it simpler still: “We love him, because he first loved us.”</p><p>That word “first” contains the logic of grace.</p><p>God’s love is not merely a reaction to ours. Ours is a response to His.</p><p>Grace therefore refuses to let us remain the explanation for God’s goodness. Eventually the explanation must be found in God Himself. His love, generosity and mercy proceed from who He is; they are not attitudes we occasionally persuade Him to adopt.</p><p>And yet many people know the vocabulary of grace while carrying an image of God that contradicts it. They believe they are saved by grace but live as though God’s daily disposition toward them must be purchased by spiritual performance. When they pray well, they feel confident before Him. When they fail, they withdraw.</p><p>Then Jesus enters the story.</p><p>John describes Him as “full of grace and truth.” He says that no one has seen God at any time, but the Son has made Him known.</p><p><em>Later Philip says to Jesus, “Lord, shew us the Father.”</em></p><p>Show us what God is actually like.</p><p><em>Jesus answers, “He that hath seen me hath seen the Father.”</em></p><p>That statement forces every picture of God to face Jesus.</p><p>A leper approaches Him in Mark 1 and says, “If thou wilt, thou canst make me clean.”</p><p>The man does not question ability. “Thou canst.” His uncertainty concerns willingness. “If thou wilt.”</p><p>Many believers know that tension. God can help me, but does He want to? God has wisdom, but will He give it to me? God has power, but what is His disposition toward me?</p><p><em>Jesus answers the man’s uncertainty directly:</em></p><p>“I will.”</p><p>Before we turn the passage into a formula about every desired outcome, notice what it reveals about Christ. The man’s question concerned willingness, and he encountered willingness.</p><p>This does not mean every prayer receives the outcome we request. Paul asked for the removal of his thorn and received a different answer. Yet even there grace reveals God.</p><p>“My grace is sufficient for thee,” the Lord told him, “for my strength is made perfect in weakness.”</p><p>Grace here is not forgiveness.</p><p>It is sufficiency.</p><p>Paul’s weakness remains real, but it is no longer the only power in the equation.</p><p>In Karachi, Pakistan, Abdul Sattar Edhi began with a small free dispensary. During a severe flu epidemic in 1957, he organized treatment camps. Over time, the work expanded into ambulance, shelter, medical and relief services serving people across social and religious lines. What became remarkable was not merely the size of the organization. It was the repeated movement toward people in need.</p><p>Actions reveal disposition.</p><p>And Scripture wants us to see God’s disposition.</p><p>The cross is the clearest place.</p><p>Romans 8:32 asks, “He that spared not his own Son, but delivered him up for us all, how shall he not with him also freely give us all things?”</p><p>Paul is reasoning from what God has already done.</p><p>Look at the cross, then draw a conclusion about God.</p><p>Fear often does the opposite. Fear looks at today’s unanswered question and reasons backward toward God’s character. The door closed, therefore perhaps God has abandoned me. The answer did not come as expected, therefore perhaps He is reluctant.</p><p>Paul reverses the process.</p><p>Begin with God’s clearest revelation.</p><p>The cross establishes something about His disposition that changing circumstances cannot be allowed to erase.</p><p>Knowing God does not mean knowing why everything happens. But “I do not understand this” and “God cannot be trusted” are not the same sentence.</p><p>Psalm 9:10 says, “They that know thy name will put their trust in thee.”</p><p>Notice the order.</p><p>Know.</p><p>Then trust.</p><p>Perhaps we have tried too hard to manufacture trust while giving too little attention to knowing the One we are being asked to trust.</p><p>Grace introduces us to God.</p><p>And as He becomes clearer, circumstances begin to lose their ability to define Him for us.</p><p>“My strength is limited” no longer means “God’s help is limited.”</p><p>“I do not know” no longer means “God does not know.”</p><p>“I cannot see a way” no longer means “there is no way.”</p><p>The facts remain facts.</p><p>They simply stop pretending to be God.</p><p>Before this book asks us to trust more, act more courageously or live more peacefully, it must ask a more fundamental question:</p><p>Who is the God we are trusting?</p><p>Grace begins our answer.</p>"},{"slug":"chapter-2-the-god-who-gives-first","kicker":"Chapter Two","title":"The God Who Gives First","body":"<p>On July 20, 2014, a sick traveller from Liberia arrived in Lagos. Dr. Ameyo Stella Adadevoh, a Nigerian physician at First Consultants Medical Centre, suspected that his illness might be Ebola. She pressed for isolation and resisted pressure to allow him to leave. Nigeria would eventually record twenty Ebola cases and eight deaths, but on October 20 the World Health Organization declared the outbreak over.</p><p>The story involved many people, institutions and difficult decisions, and it cost health workers their lives, including Adadevoh. Yet one feature stands out: action had to begin before the danger had spread everywhere.</p><p>Initiative matters.</p><p>That is one of the most important patterns in Scripture, and it tells us something essential about God. We often think of God as responding to human movement: we seek, then He appears; we improve, then He becomes gracious; we become serious, then He becomes generous.</p><p>But when Scripture tells the story from God’s side, the order repeatedly runs the other way.</p><p>“We love him, because he first loved us.”</p><p>The word “first” is doing enormous work.</p><p>John is not merely saying that God loves more strongly than we do. He is locating the beginning of the relationship. Our love is response. His love is initiative.</p><p>Grace is therefore not simply what God does after we come to Him. Grace helps explain why there is a way to come at all.</p><p>After Adam and Eve sinned in Genesis 3, they hid. God came asking, “Where art thou?”</p><p>The question was not evidence that God had misplaced them. It exposed the direction in which sin had moved humanity.</p><p>They were hiding.</p><p>God was seeking.</p><p>Even after judgment was pronounced, Genesis records that God made garments for them. Humanity had attempted to cover its shame. God moved toward the people withdrawing from Him.</p><p>The same pattern appears in Abraham’s story. Joshua 24 looks backward and says Abraham’s ancestors lived beyond the river and served other gods. Then God says, “I took your father Abraham.”</p><p>The story begins with divine initiative.</p><p>Abraham still had to respond. He had to leave. He had to walk. He had to trust. Grace does not eliminate human response. But human response did not create the call.</p><p>God spoke first.</p><p>Israel’s story follows the same pattern. In Egypt, the Israelites had no army capable of defeating Pharaoh and no political leverage strong enough to negotiate freedom. Then God told Moses that He had seen their affliction, heard their cry, knew their sorrows and had “come down to deliver them.”</p><p>Before Israel took a step toward freedom, God had already revealed Himself as Deliverer.</p><p>Israel would have to move. They would leave Egypt, walk toward the sea and follow God through the wilderness. But their movement was a response to something God had already initiated.</p><p>This tells us something about grace that becomes difficult to see when Christianity is reduced to human performance.</p><p>God is not standing at the end of a spiritual obstacle course waiting to see who can reach Him.</p><p>He is the God who comes near.</p><p>In the 1970s, India was one of the hardest fronts in the global campaign against smallpox. Health workers moved beyond simply waiting for patients to present themselves. Massive active searches were organized from village to village, cases were identified, contacts traced, outbreaks contained and vaccination intensified. India recorded its last smallpox case in May 1975.</p><p>The programme did not wait for the disease to solve itself.</p><p>It moved toward the problem.</p><p>Human public-health action is not divine grace, but it reminds us that help is most meaningful when it acts before helplessness has repaired itself.</p><p>The gospel announces precisely that kind of divine movement.</p><p>Deuteronomy 7 tells Israel that God did not set His love upon them because they were more numerous than other peoples. In fact, they were among the fewest.</p><p>Their importance could not explain His love.</p><p>The explanation had to be found in God.</p><p>That is uncomfortable to the part of us that wants to be the reason.</p><p>We like achievements we can point to. If someone chooses us because we are the most qualified, the relationship feels understandable. Maintain the qualification and perhaps we can maintain the favour.</p><p>Grace removes that foundation.</p><p>If God’s goodness begins in God, then my confidence cannot finally rest in my ability to keep giving Him reasons to remain good.</p><p>This does not make obedience irrelevant.</p><p>It puts obedience in its proper place.</p><p>Obedience becomes response rather than purchase.</p><p>Jesus makes this strikingly clear in His encounter with Zacchaeus. Luke 19 introduces Zacchaeus as a chief tax collector and a wealthy man. He climbs a tree because he wants to see Jesus. When Jesus reaches him, He says, “Zacchaeus, make haste, and come down; for to day I must abide at thy house.”</p><p>Notice the order.</p><p>Jesus does not say, “First repair everything you have done, and then I will consider entering your house.”</p><p>His presence comes before Zacchaeus announces restitution.</p><p>Later Zacchaeus declares that he will give generously to the poor and restore fourfold where he has taken falsely.</p><p>Grace did not make transformation unnecessary.</p><p>Grace entered the house before the transformation was complete.</p><p>Saul of Tarsus makes the point even more dramatically. Acts 9 does not present Saul quietly asking God to reveal Himself. He is travelling to Damascus with authority to arrest followers of Jesus.</p><p>His direction is actively wrong.</p><p>Then Christ interrupts him.</p><p>“Saul, Saul, why persecutest thou me?”</p><p>Saul’s new life begins with an encounter he did not arrange.</p><p>He must respond. He must obey. His life must change profoundly. But transformation begins because Christ meets a man travelling in the opposite direction.</p><p>This is grace as initiative.</p><p>On December 3, 1967, surgeons at Groote Schuur Hospital in Cape Town performed the world’s first human-to-human heart transplant. The donor, Denise Darvall, had suffered fatal injuries in a road accident. Her father consented to the donation of her heart. The recipient, Louis Washkansky, was desperately ill. The transplant did not guarantee the outcome he might have wanted; he died eighteen days later from pneumonia. Yet the gift came before recovery could even be possible.</p><p>The sequence matters.</p><p>Grace is not a reward handed to people who have reached the point where they no longer need it.</p><p>Romans 5 says, “when we were yet without strength, in due time Christ died for the ungodly.” A few verses later: “while we were yet sinners, Christ died for us.”</p><p>Without strength.</p><p>Ungodly.</p><p>Sinners.</p><p>Paul seems determined to remove every possibility that the cross can be explained as God’s reward for an impressive human condition.</p><p>God did not wait until humanity became worthy of rescue.</p><p>The rescue reveals what God is like.</p><p>This changes how we understand prayer, fasting, obedience and spiritual discipline.</p><p>Prayer is not a technique for persuading an unwilling God to notice us.</p><p>Fasting is not payment designed to make God generous.</p><p>Obedience is not currency with which we purchase divine affection.</p><p>These practices matter deeply, but they exist inside relationship with God. They do not manufacture God’s character.</p><p>Jesus says in Matthew 6 that the Father knows what we need before we ask Him.</p><p>Then Jesus teaches us to pray.</p><p>Prayer therefore does not inform an ignorant God.</p><p>We ask a God who already knows.</p><p>This does not mean that every request will be answered in the form we prefer. A loving God cannot be reduced to a machine that converts desire into outcomes. If He is wiser than we are, His goodness must sometimes operate beyond the answer we would have chosen.</p><p>But an answer we do not understand is not evidence that God’s character has changed.</p><p>Romans 8:32 says, “He that spared not his own Son, but delivered him up for us all…”</p><p>Paul is not asking us to trust a God who has given no evidence of His disposition.</p><p>God has already moved.</p><p>God has already given.</p><p>God has already acted.</p><p>Ephesians 2 describes us as dead in sins, “but God,” rich in mercy and great in love, made us alive with Christ. Then Paul says, “by grace ye are saved through faith.”</p><p>Faith matters.</p><p>But faith does not turn a reluctant God into a gracious one.</p><p>Faith receives what grace has made available.</p><p>And almost immediately Paul says that we are created in Christ Jesus unto good works.</p><p>Grace comes first.</p><p>Action follows.</p><p>That order protects us from two opposite errors.</p><p>The first is pride: believing our performance explains God’s goodness.</p><p>The second is passivity: believing that because grace begins with God, our response does not matter.</p><p>Scripture permits neither.</p><p>Grace initiates.</p><p>Faith receives.</p><p>Love responds.</p><p>Obedience moves.</p><p>Good works follow.</p><p>The resulting life may be extraordinarily active, but the activity grows from a different root. We are not working to persuade God to become our God. We are responding because He has revealed Himself.</p><p>God first.</p><p>That does not make our response unimportant.</p><p>It makes grace astonishing.</p><p>And perhaps peace begins to grow when we stop waking each day trying to persuade God to become the God He has already revealed Himself to be.</p>"},{"slug":"chapter-3-the-god-greater-than-our-perspective","kicker":"Chapter Three","title":"The God Greater Than Our Perspective","body":"<p>On July 31, 1996, Nigeria’s football team faced Brazil in the Olympic semifinal in Atlanta. Brazil led 3–1. The score was not an opinion. It accurately described the match at that moment. Yet Nigeria scored twice late in normal time and Nwankwo Kanu struck again in extra time to complete a 4–3 victory. Nigeria went on to win the gold medal.</p><p>At 3–1, the scoreboard was true.</p><p>It simply was not final.</p><p>Much of human fear begins when we confuse those two things.</p><p>We live so close to our circumstances that what is nearest to us often feels largest to us. A bill on the table, a diagnosis in a report, a delayed answer, a closed door, a limited account balance, an uncertain future—these things may be real, but their nearness can make them appear total.</p><p>Scripture repeatedly warns us against that mistake.</p><p>When God says in Isaiah 55, “My thoughts are not your thoughts, neither are your ways my ways,” He is not merely saying that He is somewhat smarter than we are. He is confronting the instinct to measure divine reality by human sight. “For as the heavens are higher than the earth, so are my ways higher than your ways, and my thoughts than your thoughts.”</p><p>The comparison is not small. It is meant to humble us.</p><p>We do not simply know less than God knows. We know from a position that is altogether smaller than His.</p><p>That is why grace is necessary, but so is perspective. If grace reveals that God is good, perspective teaches us why our judgment of His goodness can become distorted so quickly. We often evaluate Him from one unfinished scene.</p><p>In the nineteenth century, astronomers knew that Uranus did not move exactly as their calculations predicted. Instead of concluding that the laws of motion had failed, mathematicians Urbain Le Verrier in France and John Couch Adams in England independently calculated that the orbit might be disturbed by another planet. In September 1846, Johann Galle observed Neptune close to the predicted position.</p><p>The irregularity was real.</p><p>But the visible planet was not the only object relevant to the explanation.</p><p>That is often the condition of our understanding before God.</p><p>We observe accurately and then conclude prematurely.</p><p><em>We say, “This does not make sense.”</em></p><p>Perhaps it does not—from where we stand.</p><p><em>We say, “Nothing is happening.”</em></p><p>Perhaps nothing visible is happening.</p><p><em>We say, “I have exhausted the possibilities.”</em></p><p>Perhaps we have exhausted the possibilities visible to us.</p><p>This is one reason the Bible so often resists the tyranny of sight. Paul says in 2 Corinthians 4:18, “We look not at the things which are seen, but at the things which are not seen.”</p><p>He is not asking believers to become indifferent to the visible world. He is challenging the idea that the visible world is the deepest layer of reality.</p><p>The same pattern appears throughout Scripture.</p><p>Joseph was truly in a pit before he was in a palace.</p><p>David was truly hunted before he was enthroned.</p><p>Israel truly stood between Pharaoh and the sea.</p><p>Martha truly stood before the death of her brother.</p><p>None of those facts were illusions.</p><p>But none of them was a complete interpretation.</p><p>This is where many believers suffer unnecessary instability. We often assume that if a fact is painful, then it must also be ultimate.</p><p>But facts can be real without being ultimate.</p><p>Lazarus was truly dead. Jesus did not deny the fact. Yet the truth of Lazarus’s death was not the total truth of the situation once the Son of God stood before the tomb.</p><p>In 1995, the Hubble Space Telescope was pointed for days at a tiny patch of sky that appeared almost empty. The resulting Hubble Deep Field revealed roughly three thousand distant galaxies in that small area.</p><p>The emptiness had been an observation made from limited sight.</p><p>A deeper view revealed fullness.</p><p>That does not mean every empty-looking season contains a hidden outcome we will later celebrate. Scripture does not promise that every loss will be reversed in the form we prefer.</p><p>The point is humbler.</p><p>Our sight is not exhaustive.</p><p>A silent season may be genuinely silent to us.</p><p>A waiting period may contain no explanation we can access.</p><p>A prayer without immediate answer may leave us confused.</p><p>But what if our inability to see is evidence of our limitation rather than evidence of God’s absence?</p><p>Job did not receive every answer he wanted.</p><p>Abraham was not shown the whole story at once.</p><p>The disciples did not understand the cross while it was happening.</p><p>At several points the central question became the same: would they insist that the range of their own sight defined the range of reality?</p><p>That question is still before us.</p><p>Isaiah 46 describes God as the One “declaring the end from the beginning.”</p><p>God is never discovering information.</p><p>He is never waiting for history to surprise Him.</p><p>He is never improvising because an event developed beyond His foresight.</p><p>That does not mean all divine action is easy for us to understand.</p><p>It means God is never trapped inside the uncertainty that governs us.</p><p>This is why trust in God is fundamentally different from optimism.</p><p>Optimism often assumes things will probably work out.</p><p>Faith rests in the God who remains God whether or not I can see how things will work out.</p><p>Optimism leans on probability.</p><p>Faith leans on revelation.</p><p>In 2 Kings 6, Elisha’s servant wakes to find the city surrounded by an enemy army. His fear is understandable. The horses and chariots are really there.</p><p><em>Elisha prays, “LORD, I pray thee, open his eyes, that he may see.”</em></p><p>Then the servant sees the mountain full of horses and chariots of fire around Elisha.</p><p>The first sight was not false.</p><p>It was incomplete.</p><p>That story should not be used to suggest that believers will always be shown what God is doing behind every frightening circumstance. Most of the time, we are not.</p><p>But it establishes a principle: reality may contain more than fear can see.</p><p>That principle is not meant to make us careless.</p><p>It is meant to make us humble.</p><p>We are creatures. We live inside time, inside process, inside partial knowledge.</p><p>God does not.</p><p>Psalm 139 says there is nowhere we can go from His presence. Our future is not unexplored territory to Him. Our hidden thoughts are not hidden to Him. Our days do not surprise Him.</p><p>The God of grace is not simply good.</p><p>He is greater than our frame of reference.</p><p>This changes the way we read our lives.</p><p>We no longer begin with the assumption that what presses hardest on us is therefore most authoritative.</p><p>We learn to say, “This is what I see, but I will not assume that what I see is the whole.”</p><p><em>Honesty says, “The need is real.”</em></p><p><em>Faith adds, “My need is not greater than God.”</em></p><p><em>Honesty says, “I do not know how.”</em></p><p><em>Faith adds, “My ignorance is not God’s ignorance.”</em></p><p><em>Honesty says, “I cannot see a path.”</em></p><p>Faith adds, “The absence of a visible path is not the absence of divine wisdom.”</p><p>This is one reason peace grows through the knowledge of God.</p><p>If peace depended on my ability to see the whole, I could never possess it.</p><p>I do not see the whole.</p><p>I see fragments.</p><p>But if peace rests in the character and greatness of God, then I can be stable without being all-knowing.</p><p>Nigeria’s 3–1 deficit was real without being final. Uranus’s irregular motion was real without being fully explained by what astronomers could already see. A seemingly empty patch of sky was real to ordinary sight while containing thousands of galaxies beyond that sight.</p><p>None of these events proves Scripture.</p><p><em>They simply remind us of something Scripture already insists upon:</em></p><p>The limits of our sight must never be mistaken for the limits of reality.</p><p>And if that is true within creation, how much more when we are speaking of the Creator?</p><p>The question now becomes whether our interpretation of life will be ruled by what is nearest to us or by what God has revealed about Himself.</p><p>If we know Him only through our circumstances, He will seem to change every week.</p><p>If we know Him through His revelation, we will begin to see our circumstances in a new light.</p><p>That is where a deeper peace begins—not in seeing everything, but in knowing the God whose sight is never limited.</p>"},{"slug":"chapter-4-the-god-who-is-enough","kicker":"Chapter Four","title":"The God Who Is Enough","body":"<p>In March 2007, Kenya’s Safaricom launched M-PESA, a mobile-phone service that allowed people to send and receive money through simple handsets and a network of local agents. At the time, many Kenyans had limited access to conventional banks. M-PESA did not begin by constructing a bank branch in every village. It used something already widespread—the mobile phone—and created another channel through which financial services could move.</p><p>The absence of one kind of access did not mean the absence of every possible channel.</p><p>That distinction matters because we often interpret the loss of a channel as the loss of supply itself.</p><p>A job ends, and we say provision has ended.</p><p>A person who helped us leaves, and we conclude that help has left with the person.</p><p>A plan fails, and suddenly the future appears empty.</p><p>We become so accustomed to receiving through particular channels that we quietly begin treating the channel as the source.</p><p>Then the channel closes.</p><p>And panic reveals what we had actually been trusting.</p><p>The previous chapter brought us to the limits of human perspective. But that creates another question. If reality is greater than what we presently see, what exactly are we trusting beyond our sight?</p><p><em>Christian faith does not answer, “Something will work out.”</em></p><p>It answers with a Person.</p><p>God.</p><p>And Scripture does not merely reveal Him as possessing more resources than we possess. It reveals Him as sufficient in Himself.</p><p>When God introduced Himself to Moses in Exodus 3, He used the extraordinary name, “I AM THAT I AM.”</p><p><em>God does not say, “I became.”</em></p><p><em>He does not say, “I am because something else sustains Me.”</em></p><p>He simply is.</p><p>Everything else we know exists dependently. Plants need soil, water and light. Human beings need oxygen, food, water and countless conditions we did not create. Nations need economies. Machines require energy. Even the sun consumes fuel.</p><p>Creation is a web of dependency.</p><p>Then Scripture introduces us to One who does not belong inside that chain of dependence.</p><p>God is not being supplied by creation.</p><p>Creation is being sustained by God.</p><p>That matters because when we speak about God’s sufficiency, we are not merely saying that God owns many things.</p><p>A wealthy person can possess enormous resources and still depend on food, health, oxygen and time.</p><p>God’s sufficiency is not simply abundance.</p><p>It is freedom from the deficiencies that govern created beings.</p><p>Psalm 50 uses startling language. God tells Israel that if He were hungry, He would not need to tell them, because the world and its fullness belong to Him.</p><p>The point is not that God experiences hunger.</p><p>The language exposes the absurdity of imagining that worship exists because God needs humanity to supply something missing in Him.</p><p>He does not need our strength to become strong.</p><p>He does not need our wisdom to become wise.</p><p>He does not need our resources to become rich.</p><p>God is not waiting for creation to complete Him.</p><p>This is where grace becomes astonishing.</p><p>The God who needs nothing chooses to give.</p><p>His involvement with us is not driven by deficiency in Him.</p><p>It flows from fullness.</p><p>In June 1948, road and rail routes into West Berlin were cut off by the Soviet blockade. The obvious channels had closed. The Western Allies responded by supplying the city by air. Over fifteen months, the Berlin Airlift carried more than two million tons of food, coal and other supplies into the city.</p><p>The roads were closed.</p><p>Supply itself was not exhausted.</p><p>Human history is full of solutions that arrived through channels people had not initially expected. Scripture takes the point further: no channel is God.</p><p>A salary is not God.</p><p>A business is not God.</p><p>A customer is not God.</p><p>An institution is not God.</p><p>A particular person is not God.</p><p>These may be instruments through which God’s provision reaches us, but confusing a channel with the Source gives the channel a place it cannot safely occupy.</p><p>Paul learned something similar in a more personal way.</p><p>In 2 Corinthians 12, he speaks about a “thorn in the flesh.” He asked the Lord three times for it to depart.</p><p><em>The answer was:</em></p><p>“My grace is sufficient for thee: for my strength is made perfect in weakness.”</p><p>Paul presented weakness.</p><p>God answered with sufficiency.</p><p>He did not tell Paul that weakness was imaginary.</p><p>He did not even remove it in the manner Paul requested.</p><p>Instead, God revealed something about Himself that changed the meaning of Paul’s weakness.</p><p>“My grace is sufficient.”</p><p>The sufficiency belonged to God before Paul discovered his insufficiency.</p><p>Paul’s crisis did not create grace.</p><p>It exposed his need for what was already present in God.</p><p>We often imagine that God becomes sufficient when our resources run out.</p><p>He does not.</p><p>He was sufficient while our resources were still abundant.</p><p>We simply noticed ourselves more.</p><p>In August 2010, a collapse at Chile’s San José mine trapped thirty-three miners about 2,300 feet underground. They were found alive seventeen days later. For weeks, narrow boreholes became channels for food, water, communication and medical support while a rescue shaft was prepared. Sixty-nine days after the collapse, all thirty-three were brought to the surface alive.</p><p>The underground chamber did not suddenly become abundant.</p><p>What changed was connection to supply beyond it.</p><p>That distinction helps us understand grace.</p><p>God does not always remove the confined space immediately.</p><p>Sometimes His sufficiency reaches us inside it.</p><p>Paul’s prison did not have to disappear before grace could sustain him.</p><p>Israel’s wilderness did not have to become Canaan before God could feed them.</p><p>David’s cave did not have to become a palace before God could preserve him.</p><p>The disciples did not need an unlimited food supply in their hands before Jesus could ask them what they already had.</p><p>We naturally expect sufficiency to look like abundance around us.</p><p>Scripture often reveals it first as abundance in God.</p><p>This is why Psalm 23 begins with a declaration about the Shepherd before it describes the circumstances of the sheep:</p><p>“The LORD is my shepherd; I shall not want.”</p><p>David’s confidence is not anchored first in the pasture.</p><p>It is anchored in the Shepherd.</p><p>The psalm contains green pastures, but it also contains the valley of the shadow of death.</p><p>The Shepherd is sufficient in both places.</p><p>If our theology of God’s sufficiency works only in green pastures, then we have confused the pasture with God.</p><p>Paul makes a similar discovery in Philippians 4. He says he has learned how to live with abundance and how to suffer need. Then comes the famous statement, “I can do all things through Christ which strengtheneth me.”</p><p>The verse is often used as though Paul were announcing unlimited personal achievement.</p><p>His argument is almost the opposite.</p><p>Paul is saying that changing circumstances do not have final authority over his capacity to remain faithful.</p><p>Plenty does not sustain him.</p><p>Scarcity does not destroy him.</p><p>Christ strengthens him in both.</p><p>This does not mean having everything we would prefer.</p><p>Sometimes it means discovering that God remains enough when we do not.</p><p>That understanding changes action.</p><p>I do not have to possess tomorrow’s resources today in order to obey God today.</p><p>I do not have to understand the entire path before I use the light I have.</p><p>I do not have to pretend five loaves are more than five.</p><p>I only need to stop treating five loaves as the full measure of what God can do.</p><p>M-PESA reminds us that a missing conventional channel is not the same as the absence of all possibility. The Berlin Airlift reminds us that a blocked route is not identical to exhausted supply. The Chilean miners remind us that severe restriction can coexist with sustaining help from beyond the restricted environment.</p><p>None of these events proves a theological doctrine.</p><p>Scripture gives us something greater.</p><p>The God who says, “I AM,” cannot be measured by what is presently in our hands.</p><p>The God whose grace was sufficient for Paul has not become insufficient because our circumstances have changed.</p><p>Perhaps the greatest shift is therefore not from having little to having much.</p><p>It is from seeing our resources as the centre of reality to seeing God as the centre.</p><p><em>Then we can say honestly:</em></p><p>This may not be enough for everything.</p><p>But God is enough for the next faithful step.</p><p>And no limitation can reduce Him.</p>"},{"slug":"chapter-5-the-god-who-helps","kicker":"Chapter Five","title":"The God Who Helps","body":"<p>In 2003, parts of northern Nigeria suspended polio vaccination amid fears and mistrust about the oral vaccine. The consequences were serious, and wild poliovirus continued to circulate. Years of work followed: vaccination campaigns, surveillance, laboratory work, negotiations with communities, involvement of traditional and religious leaders, health workers reaching difficult areas, and repeated responses to setbacks. In 2016, four new wild-polio cases were found after Nigeria had gone two years without one. The work continued. In August 2020, the World Health Organization’s African Region was certified free of wild poliovirus, with Nigeria’s progress making the continental milestone possible.</p><p>Help had not arrived as one dramatic moment.</p><p>It came as persistence, knowledge, people, systems, correction and repeated action.</p><p>That matters because we are comfortable calling God powerful. We are often less certain about calling Him Helper.</p><p>Power tells us what someone can do.</p><p>Help tells us something about what that person is willing to do with the power.</p><p>The distinction matters.</p><p>A person in deep water does not merely need to know that someone on the bank is strong. The question that matters is whether that strength will move toward him.</p><p>Many believers live with precisely that tension before God.</p><p>We know He can.</p><p>Can God provide? Of course.</p><p>Can God give wisdom? Certainly.</p><p>Can God strengthen the weak? Scripture says He can.</p><p><em>But somewhere underneath those convictions sits another question:</em></p><p>Will He help me?</p><p>That question takes us deeper into the knowledge of God.</p><p>Psalm 46 does not merely describe God as powerful. It calls Him “our refuge and strength, a very present help in trouble.”</p><p>The wording matters.</p><p>Not merely power.</p><p>Help.</p><p>Not merely help somewhere in the universe.</p><p>Present help.</p><p>And not only when trouble has disappeared.</p><p>Help in trouble.</p><p>That tells us something about God that circumstances alone cannot teach us.</p><p>We often expect divine help to be proved by the absence of difficulty. If God helped me, perhaps the trouble should never have happened. If God were present, perhaps I would not have entered the crisis.</p><p>But the psalm places God and trouble in the same sentence.</p><p>God is present help in trouble.</p><p>Joseph had God with him in Potiphar’s house, but Genesis 39 also says the Lord was with Joseph in prison.</p><p>God’s presence did not mean Joseph never entered confinement.</p><p>It meant confinement did not place Joseph beyond God’s presence.</p><p>David knew God as Shepherd, yet Psalm 23 still contains a valley of the shadow of death.</p><p>Paul knew grace, yet experienced weakness.</p><p>The disciples had Jesus in the boat, yet encountered a storm.</p><p>Our theology becomes fragile whenever we require God’s presence to look only like prevention.</p><p>Sometimes God helps by preventing.</p><p>Sometimes He helps by providing.</p><p>Sometimes He helps by giving wisdom.</p><p>Sometimes by sending people.</p><p>Sometimes by strengthening us to endure what He does not immediately remove.</p><p>Sometimes by showing us what to do with something already in our hands.</p><p>These forms are different, but they lead us toward the same question: what kind of God involves Himself with human need?</p><p>Scripture’s answer is remarkable.</p><p><em>In Isaiah 41:10, God says:</em></p><p>“Fear thou not; for I am with thee… I will strengthen thee; yea, I will help thee.”</p><p>Again, the language is personal.</p><p>“I will help thee.”</p><p>The God revealed in Scripture is not merely sufficient in Himself. His sufficiency moves outward.</p><p>That is grace.</p><p>In May and June 1940, hundreds of thousands of Allied troops were trapped around Dunkirk as German forces advanced through France. Operation Dynamo used naval vessels, merchant ships and many smaller craft to evacuate more than 338,000 troops.</p><p>Those soldiers could not rescue themselves simply by becoming more determined.</p><p>Help had to reach their position.</p><p>Being unable to rescue yourself does not mean rescue cannot come.</p><p>Scripture takes that simple observation beyond human examples.</p><p><em>Psalm 121 asks:</em></p><p>“From whence cometh my help?”</p><p><em>Then answers:</em></p><p>“My help cometh from the LORD, which made heaven and earth.”</p><p>The Helper is the Creator.</p><p>The One willing to become involved is the One whose resources do not end where ours end.</p><p>James 1 gives a particularly important example of how that help may arrive:</p><p>“If any of you lack wisdom, let him ask of God.”</p><p>Notice the problem.</p><p>Lack.</p><p>But God’s answer to lack is not always the immediate appearance of more material resources.</p><p>Sometimes God gives wisdom.</p><p>And wisdom can change what existing resources are able to accomplish.</p><p>A person may ask for more money when what is first required is better judgment concerning the money already available.</p><p>Someone may ask for another opportunity while failing to recognize the opportunity already present.</p><p>Another may pray for deliverance from a problem when wisdom concerning how to address the problem is part of the help being offered.</p><p>This does not mean every difficulty can be solved by cleverness.</p><p>It means God’s help cannot be reduced to one form.</p><p>In June 2018, twelve boys from a football team and their coach became trapped by floodwater inside Thailand’s Tham Luang cave system. The eventual rescue involved Thai authorities and volunteers, elite cave divers from several countries, medical specialists, military personnel, engineers and hundreds of others. All thirteen trapped people were brought out alive.</p><p>Finding them was help.</p><p>Food was help.</p><p>Medical care was help.</p><p>Engineering was help.</p><p>Diving expertise was help.</p><p>Coordination was help.</p><p>One crisis required many forms of assistance.</p><p>That helps expose a limitation in the way we sometimes think about God.</p><p>We want the miracle to arrive already assembled.</p><p>But God’s help in Scripture often has several parts.</p><p>Consider Elijah during famine in 1 Kings 17. At one point ravens bring him food. Later a widow becomes part of his provision. The source remains God.</p><p>The channels change.</p><p>If we identify God too closely with one channel, the loss of that channel will feel like the loss of God.</p><p>A salary can be a channel.</p><p>It is not God.</p><p>A particular employer can be a channel.</p><p>It is not God.</p><p>A relationship can become a channel of help.</p><p>It is not the Source.</p><p>This does not make people unnecessary. God often helps people through people. Scripture teaches generosity, community, counsel and bearing one another’s burdens.</p><p>But there is freedom in knowing the difference between receiving help through someone and making that person the foundation of our confidence.</p><p>Channels may change.</p><p>The Helper remains.</p><p>Hebrews 13 brings this into focus. God says, “I will never leave thee, nor forsake thee,” and the writer immediately draws a conclusion:</p><p>“So that we may boldly say, The Lord is my helper.”</p><p>Notice what produces boldness.</p><p>Not certainty that a particular human channel will remain.</p><p>The certainty that God will not abandon.</p><p>This knowledge should also change the way we respond to resources already available.</p><p>If God is Helper, then faith is not merely waiting for Him to act while refusing to move ourselves.</p><p>Divine help and human action repeatedly meet in Scripture.</p><p>Noah builds.</p><p>Abraham travels.</p><p>Moses stretches out the rod.</p><p>Israel walks.</p><p>The priests step toward the Jordan.</p><p>The servants fill waterpots at Cana.</p><p>The disciples distribute the bread.</p><p>Paul works.</p><p>Grace does not erase participation.</p><p>It makes participation possible without making us the ultimate source of the outcome.</p><p>That is why courage can grow from knowing God.</p><p>I can act with limited knowledge while asking the God who gives wisdom.</p><p>I can begin with limited resources while trusting the God whose sufficiency is not limited by them.</p><p>I can ask another person for assistance without making that person my god.</p><p>I can revise a failed plan without concluding that God has failed.</p><p>I can admit that I need help without treating need as shame.</p><p>Dependence is not an embarrassment in Scripture.</p><p>It is part of being created.</p><p>Only God is self-sufficient.</p><p>Human beings were never designed to occupy His place.</p><p>Perhaps some of our anxiety comes from secretly trying to do exactly that.</p><p>We want enough information to guarantee the future.</p><p>Enough money to eliminate vulnerability.</p><p>Enough influence to ensure that no important door can close.</p><p>Enough strength never to need anyone.</p><p>But Scripture offers something different.</p><p>It offers God.</p><p>A God who knows that we are creatures.</p><p>A God who does not shame us for needing wisdom.</p><p>A God who invites us to ask.</p><p>A God who calls Himself our refuge, strength and present help.</p><p>Knowing that God changes what it means to ask for help.</p><p>It is no longer merely the final act of a desperate person.</p><p>It can become the ordinary response of someone who knows Him.</p>"},{"slug":"chapter-6-the-god-who-works-with-what-is-in-your-hand","kicker":"Chapter Six","title":"The God Who Works With What Is in Your Hand","body":"<p>In 1989, Nigerian physician Otu Oviemo Ovadje developed what became known as the Emergency Auto-Transfusion Set, or EAT-SET. The problem was urgent: in settings where banked blood was scarce, patients with severe internal bleeding could die while the blood they had already lost collected inside their own bodies. The device was designed to recover and filter a patient’s own blood for reinfusion.</p><p>The striking idea was not that a new supply suddenly appeared from somewhere else.</p><p>A resource already present was recognized, recovered and used differently.</p><p>That does not make every shortage a hidden abundance. But it does expose a habit of mind: we often look past what is available because it does not resemble what we wish we had.</p><p>That pattern appears repeatedly in Scripture.</p><p>When God met Moses in Exodus 4, Moses was preoccupied with what he lacked. He questioned his credibility, his ability to speak and the likelihood that Israel would believe him.</p><p><em>Then God asked him a strangely ordinary question:</em></p><p>“What is that in thine hand?”</p><p><em>Moses answered:</em></p><p>“A rod.”</p><p>It was not a sword.</p><p>Not an army.</p><p>Not political influence.</p><p>Not wealth.</p><p>A shepherd’s staff.</p><p>God was not asking because He needed to identify the object. He was redirecting Moses’s attention.</p><p>Moses was looking at what he did not have.</p><p>God began with what he did.</p><p>This does not mean the rod contained some hidden supernatural property. It remained wood. Its significance came from the God who chose to work through it.</p><p>The power was never in Moses’s possession.</p><p>The power was in God’s presence.</p><p>Yet God still asked for what was in Moses’s hand.</p><p>This reveals something important about the God of grace. He does not always begin by replacing everything we have with something more impressive. Sometimes He begins by changing what becomes possible through what is already present.</p><p>We often assume divine provision must look like addition.</p><p>More money.</p><p>More people.</p><p>More equipment.</p><p>More influence.</p><p>More opportunity.</p><p>And sometimes God does provide more.</p><p>But Scripture also reveals a God who asks, in effect, “What do you have?”</p><p>That question appears again in 2 Kings 4.</p><p>A widow approaches Elisha in desperate circumstances. Her husband has died. Debt remains. A creditor threatens to take her sons.</p><p><em>Elisha asks:</em></p><p>“What hast thou in the house?”</p><p><em>Her first response is revealing:</em></p><p>“Thine handmaid hath not any thing in the house, save a pot of oil.”</p><p>Nothing.</p><p>Except.</p><p>Those two ideas sit beside each other.</p><p>Nothing—except oil.</p><p>To the widow, the oil belongs almost in the category of nothing because it appears hopelessly inadequate beside the size of the problem.</p><p>But the story changes around what she nearly discounted.</p><p>The oil is poured into borrowed vessels until there are no more vessels available. She sells the oil, pays the debt and lives with her children on what remains.</p><p>The lesson is not that every small possession will miraculously multiply if we perform the correct religious action.</p><p>The deeper revelation is about God.</p><p>Human beings evaluate resources by their visible size.</p><p>God is not limited by that evaluation.</p><p>Something can be inadequate in our hands and still be enough for God to begin with.</p><p>India’s Mars Orbiter Mission offers a modern illustration of disciplined resourcefulness. Launched in 2013, the spacecraft entered Mars orbit in September 2014. The mission was executed with a government-approved budget of about 450 crore rupees, modest by the standards of interplanetary missions, and used Indian launch, spacecraft and scientific capabilities. India became the first nation to reach Mars orbit on its maiden Mars mission.</p><p>The achievement did not happen because engineers pretended limitations did not exist.</p><p>They designed within them.</p><p>That is important.</p><p>Faith does not glorify poor planning.</p><p>It does not call carelessness courage.</p><p>It does not turn limited resources into an excuse for limited thinking.</p><p>Sometimes one of the ways God helps is by giving wisdom concerning what is already available.</p><p><em>James 1:5 says:</em></p><p>“If any of you lack wisdom, let him ask of God.”</p><p>God’s provision therefore cannot be reduced to money.</p><p>An idea can be provision.</p><p>Understanding can be provision.</p><p>Skill can be provision.</p><p>Timing can be provision.</p><p>Correction can be provision.</p><p>A person who sees only material supply may be surrounded by help and still conclude that nothing has arrived.</p><p>This is one reason knowing God matters more than learning formulas for success.</p><p>If the principle becomes merely “use what you have,” the chapter can easily become self-help.</p><p>Scripture is saying something greater.</p><p>The question is not simply what is in your hand.</p><p>The question is who is with the hand.</p><p>David’s sling does not explain Goliath’s defeat.</p><p>Moses’s rod does not explain the Exodus.</p><p>The widow’s oil does not explain its multiplication.</p><p>Five loaves do not explain thousands being fed.</p><p>The visible instrument is deliberately insufficient to explain the final result.</p><p>The story keeps pointing beyond the resource toward God.</p><p>In 1799, French soldiers in Egypt discovered a broken inscribed stone near the town of Rashid, or Rosetta. The stone carried the same decree in three scripts, including Greek. Because scholars could read the Greek, the fragment became a crucial key in the eventual decipherment of Egyptian hieroglyphs.</p><p>A broken piece of stone became a doorway into a civilization’s written history.</p><p>Sometimes what looks like a fragment becomes a key.</p><p>That matters because we frequently despise incomplete things.</p><p>A partial idea.</p><p>One useful relationship.</p><p>A small amount of money.</p><p>A skill that seems ordinary.</p><p>A room instead of a building.</p><p>One customer instead of a market.</p><p>One opportunity instead of a platform.</p><p>Our minds compare what exists with the finished picture we desire, and because the two are far apart, we conclude that what exists is useless.</p><p>But Scripture repeatedly introduces us to a God who does not despise beginnings.</p><p><em>Zechariah 4:10 asks, “For who hath despised the day of small things?”</em></p><p>The question confronts our obsession with impressive beginnings.</p><p>Again, smallness is not magical.</p><p>A seed left permanently in a bag does not become a harvest because it is small.</p><p>Resources still require obedience, wisdom, work and patience.</p><p>Grace is not permission to romanticize inadequacy.</p><p>It is permission to stop treating inadequacy as sovereignty.</p><p>This is why the feeding of the multitude in John 6 is so significant.</p><p>Jesus asks Philip where bread can be bought for the crowd. Philip calculates the problem. Andrew then mentions a boy with five barley loaves and two small fish, but immediately adds:</p><p>“What are they among so many?”</p><p>That question contains the logic of scarcity.</p><p>He has identified a real resource and dismissed it in the same sentence.</p><p>There is food.</p><p>But what is it compared with the need?</p><p>That is often where fear ends the conversation.</p><p>Faith allows God to continue it.</p><p>Jesus takes the loaves.</p><p>He gives thanks.</p><p>He distributes.</p><p>The crowd eats.</p><p>The miracle is not a celebration of five loaves.</p><p>It is a revelation of Jesus.</p><p>The loaves tell us what was available.</p><p>The multiplication tells us who was present.</p><p>That distinction should govern how we think about every resource God places in our hands.</p><p>The resource matters.</p><p>God matters more.</p><p><em>Paul captures the balance in 2 Corinthians 4:7:</em></p><p>“We have this treasure in earthen vessels, that the excellency of the power may be of God, and not of us.”</p><p>Earthen vessels are ordinary.</p><p>Fragile.</p><p>Unimpressive.</p><p>Yet God deliberately places treasure in them.</p><p>Why?</p><p>So that the explanation remains clear.</p><p>The vessel participates.</p><p>The power belongs to God.</p><p>This changes the way limitation feels.</p><p>If God only worked through people who already possessed everything required for their assignments, Moses would have been right to withdraw.</p><p>If God only worked through abundance, the widow’s oil would have been irrelevant.</p><p>If God only worked through impressive resources, the boy’s lunch should never have been mentioned.</p><p>But the God revealed in Scripture repeatedly begins in places where human beings would hesitate to begin.</p><p>This does not guarantee that everything we attempt will succeed.</p><p>An idea can be bad.</p><p>A business can fail.</p><p>A plan can require revision.</p><p>A door can genuinely be wrong for us.</p><p>Knowing God is not believing He is obligated to multiply whatever we choose.</p><p>It is knowing that insufficiency does not disqualify us from obedience when He has called us to move.</p><p><em>Perhaps that is why God’s question to Moses remains so powerful:</em></p><p>“What is that in thine hand?”</p><p>The question does not deny what Moses lacks.</p><p>It prevents what Moses lacks from becoming the only thing Moses sees.</p><p>The Nigerian doctor saw a possible resource in blood that would otherwise be lost. Indian engineers reached toward Mars by working carefully within real constraints. A broken Egyptian stone became a key to a language.</p><p>None proves a biblical doctrine.</p><p>They simply remind us how often human beings underestimate what is already present because it does not yet resemble the finished result.</p><p>Scripture takes the truth further.</p><p>What is presently in your hand does not have to contain within itself the explanation for everything God may do through your obedience.</p><p>The resource can be small.</p><p>God does not become small with it.</p><p>The beginning can be unimpressive.</p><p>God does not become unimpressive with it.</p><p>So perhaps the right response to limitation is not always to ask first, “Why don’t I have more?”</p><p><em>Sometimes knowing God teaches us to ask another question:</em></p><p>“What has He already placed in my hand, and what does faithfulness require me to do with it?”</p>"},{"slug":"chapter-7-the-god-revealed-in-jesus","kicker":"Chapter Seven","title":"The God Revealed in Jesus","body":"<p>In 1876, Mary Slessor arrived in Calabar, in what is now Cross River State, Nigeria. Over the years she learned Efik, lived among the people with whom she worked, cared for vulnerable children and became especially remembered for protecting twins and challenging the killing of twins where the practice persisted. Whatever limitations belong to any nineteenth-century missionary story, one feature of her life remains striking: she chose proximity.</p><p>It is one thing to express concern about suffering from a distance.</p><p>It is another to move toward it.</p><p>That becomes enormously important when we come to Jesus, because Christianity makes a claim far greater than that Jesus merely taught people about God.</p><p><em>John writes:</em></p><p>“No man hath seen God at any time; the only begotten Son… he hath declared him.”</p><p>Human beings had asked questions about God for generations.</p><p>What is He like?</p><p>Is He distant?</p><p>Is He angry?</p><p>Is He compassionate?</p><p>What does holiness look like when it encounters failure?</p><p>What does divine power do when it meets weakness?</p><p>How does God respond when the rejected approach Him?</p><p>Then John says the Word became flesh.</p><p>God did not merely send another description.</p><p>He gave humanity a revelation.</p><p>This is why Jesus’s words to Philip in John 14 are so extraordinary. Philip says:</p><p>“Lord, shew us the Father.”</p><p><em>Jesus answers:</em></p><p>“He that hath seen me hath seen the Father.”</p><p>If we want to know God, then, we cannot construct Him primarily from our fears, experiences or assumptions.</p><p>We have to look at Jesus.</p><p>This does not mean the Father and the Son are the same Person. Christian faith distinguishes Father, Son and Holy Spirit. But Jesus reveals the Father’s nature so faithfully that Hebrews calls Him “the express image of his person,” while Colossians describes Him as “the image of the invisible God.”</p><p>So watch Jesus.</p><p>A leper approaches Him in Mark 1.</p><p><em>The man comes with a question about willingness:</em></p><p>“If thou wilt, thou canst make me clean.”</p><p>Jesus does something remarkable before the healing is even complete.</p><p>He touches him.</p><p>For everyone else, the diseased man is someone from whom distance must be maintained.</p><p>Jesus moves toward him.</p><p><em>Then He says:</em></p><p>“I will.”</p><p>If Jesus reveals God, then this encounter teaches us something.</p><p>Divine holiness is not frightened by human brokenness.</p><p>God’s purity does not make Him incapable of compassion.</p><p>Grace does not mean God becomes indifferent to what is wrong. It means His holiness does not prevent Him from moving toward people who need restoration.</p><p>That same pattern appears repeatedly.</p><p>Jesus eats with tax collectors.</p><p>He speaks publicly with a Samaritan woman whose life is complicated.</p><p>He allows children to come near when His disciples think they are an interruption.</p><p>He stops for blind Bartimaeus while a crowd is moving around Him.</p><p>He weeps at Lazarus’s tomb.</p><p>He feeds hungry people.</p><p>Again and again, divine power appears beside divine compassion.</p><p>In June 1859, Swiss businessman Henry Dunant encountered the aftermath of the Battle of Solferino in northern Italy. Thousands of wounded soldiers lay with inadequate care. Dunant helped organize local civilians to assist wounded men regardless of which army they belonged to. He later wrote about what he had seen, and his ideas helped lead to the formation of the International Committee of the Red Cross and the first Geneva Convention.</p><p>Compassion moved toward suffering and became action.</p><p>That principle helps us understand something about Jesus.</p><p><em>The Gospels sometimes tell us that He was “moved with compassion.”</em></p><p>But compassion in Jesus rarely stops at emotion.</p><p>He sees the hungry and feeds.</p><p>He sees the sick and acts.</p><p>He encounters the grieving and responds.</p><p>He sees crowds described as sheep without a shepherd and begins teaching them.</p><p>The compassion of Christ has movement in it.</p><p>And if Jesus reveals the Father, we cannot continue imagining God as emotionally indifferent to human suffering.</p><p>This does not answer every question about why suffering exists.</p><p>The presence of compassion does not mean we possess a complete explanation for pain.</p><p>But the face of Jesus prevents us from concluding that God watches suffering with cold detachment.</p><p>The incarnation makes the argument stronger still.</p><p>“The Word was made flesh, and dwelt among us.”</p><p>God did not reveal His heart merely by sending instructions from a safe distance.</p><p>In Jesus, He came near.</p><p>Philippians 2 describes Christ as taking the form of a servant and humbling Himself.</p><p>The movement of grace is downward.</p><p>Toward us.</p><p>That is completely different from the image of a God waiting at a distance for humanity to climb high enough to reach Him.</p><p>The gospel announces that God came down.</p><p>Not because humanity had become impressive.</p><p>Because God is gracious.</p><p>In 1951, Indian social reformer Baba Amte established Anandwan, a community built around the rehabilitation and dignity of people affected by leprosy. Early accounts describe an austere beginning: a small amount of money, rough land, his family and a handful of patients. Amte did not merely advocate for people whom society feared and excluded. He lived and worked among them, helping build a community where those treated as outcasts could work, learn and recover dignity.</p><p>Again, no human being can define God.</p><p>But proximity reveals something words alone cannot.</p><p>Jesus does not simply speak about the unwanted.</p><p>He eats with them.</p><p>He touches them.</p><p>He receives them.</p><p>This is why the incarnation is not an ornament in Christian theology.</p><p>It is central to knowing God.</p><p>Then we come to the cross.</p><p>Everything we have said about grace converges there.</p><p>It is possible to talk about God’s love in abstract language.</p><p>The cross gives the word love historical weight.</p><p><em>Romans 5:8 says:</em></p><p>“God commendeth his love toward us, in that, while we were yet sinners, Christ died for us.”</p><p>God does not merely announce love.</p><p>He demonstrates it.</p><p>The cross therefore becomes evidence concerning God’s character.</p><p><em>This is why Romans 8:32 reasons from it:</em></p><p>“He that spared not his own Son, but delivered him up for us all…”</p><p>Paul wants the believer to look backward at what God has already done and allow that action to shape what he believes about God’s disposition.</p><p>When fear whispers, “Perhaps God does not care,” the cross stands in the argument.</p><p>When shame says, “Perhaps He wants nothing to do with people like me,” Jesus eating with sinners complicates that conclusion.</p><p>When weakness says, “Perhaps God only works with strong people,” look at the disciples Jesus chose.</p><p>When failure says, “Perhaps one terrible mistake ends everything,” look at Peter.</p><p>Peter’s story may be one of the clearest revelations of grace in the Gospels.</p><p>He confidently declares that even if others fall away, he will remain.</p><p>Then, within hours, he denies Jesus three times.</p><p>The failure is not hidden.</p><p>But neither is what happens afterward.</p><p>After the resurrection, John 21 records Jesus meeting Peter beside the Sea of Galilee.</p><p>There is food.</p><p>There is conversation.</p><p><em>There is a question repeated three times:</em></p><p>“Lovest thou me?”</p><p>And then there is responsibility.</p><p>“Feed my sheep.”</p><p>Jesus does not pretend Peter never failed.</p><p>But failure is not allowed to become Peter’s final identity.</p><p>Grace restores.</p><p>This teaches us something about God that many people desperately need to know.</p><p>God’s knowledge of our failure is complete before His relationship with us begins.</p><p>Peter’s denial did not surprise Jesus.</p><p>Jesus predicted it.</p><p><em>Yet Jesus had also said:</em></p><p>“I have prayed for thee… and when thou art converted, strengthen thy brethren.”</p><p>Before Peter fell, Jesus could see beyond the fall.</p><p>God never loved a fictional version of you.</p><p>He knows.</p><p>And grace means His knowledge does not prevent Him from calling, correcting, restoring and working through people.</p><p>This is where knowing Jesus changes trust.</p><p>We are no longer being asked to trust an undefined supernatural power.</p><p>We have seen something of God’s character.</p><p>We have watched Him in Christ.</p><p>We have seen how Jesus treats the weak.</p><p>How He responds to the rejected.</p><p>How He handles failure.</p><p>How He exercises authority.</p><p>How He serves.</p><p>How He forgives.</p><p>How He gives Himself.</p><p>Eventually the question changes.</p><p>Instead of continually asking, “Can God be trusted?” we begin asking whether the God we fear is actually the God Jesus has revealed.</p><p>Perhaps some of our anxiety has survived because our image of God has been assembled from everything except Christ.</p><p>A harsh authority figure.</p><p>A disappointing parent.</p><p>A manipulative religious leader.</p><p>A season of unanswered questions.</p><p>A painful event.</p><p>Our own shame.</p><p>These experiences are real.</p><p>But none of them has the authority to reveal God more accurately than Jesus.</p><p>If we want to know whether God is gracious, look at Christ.</p><p>If we want to know whether God moves toward broken people, look at Christ.</p><p>If we want to know whether strength and tenderness can exist together in God, look at Christ.</p><p>If we want to know whether God gives Himself rather than merely giving things, look at the cross.</p><p>Grace is not merely something God occasionally does.</p><p>Jesus shows us what the God of grace is like.</p>"},{"slug":"chapter-8-the-god-you-can-trust","kicker":"Chapter Eight","title":"The God You Can Trust","body":"<p>In early June 1944, the Allied invasion of Normandy had been planned for June 5. Weather conditions were critical, and commanders could not personally determine what the atmosphere over the English Channel would do. Group Captain James Stagg led the meteorological team advising them. He recommended postponement because conditions on June 5 would be unsuitable, then identified a narrow and imperfect weather window for June 6. The invasion proceeded that day.</p><p>The commanders had responsibility for the decision.</p><p>They did not have equal knowledge of every factor informing it.</p><p>Trust does not require equal knowledge.</p><p>In fact, trust becomes necessary precisely because knowledge is unequal.</p><p>That brings us to the heart of faith.</p><p><em>Psalm 9:10 says:</em></p><p>“They that know thy name will put their trust in thee.”</p><p>Notice again the order.</p><p>Knowledge precedes trust.</p><p>Scripture does not present faith primarily as forcing ourselves to feel confident about uncertain circumstances. Trust grows from knowledge of the One being trusted.</p><p>This is why knowing God has been the argument of this entire book.</p><p>If we know only that God is powerful, we may still fear what He will do with His power.</p><p>If we know only that God knows everything, we may still wonder whether His knowledge is working for us or against us.</p><p>If we know only that God is present, we may still be uncertain about His disposition.</p><p>But grace has been revealing something more.</p><p>The God who is powerful is good.</p><p>The God who knows is gracious.</p><p>The God who is present is Helper.</p><p>The God who needs nothing gives.</p><p>The God who sees our weakness does not necessarily withdraw from it.</p><p>The God revealed in Jesus moves toward people.</p><p>Trust begins to become reasonable when these truths cease to be isolated doctrines and become our knowledge of a Person.</p><p><em>Paul says in 2 Timothy 1:12:</em></p><p>“I know whom I have believed.”</p><p><em>Not merely, “I know what I believe.”</em></p><p>Whom.</p><p>Christian faith is personal before it is circumstantial.</p><p>Paul’s confidence was not that he possessed enough information to predict every event in his future. His life contained imprisonment, opposition, uncertainty, physical suffering and repeated danger.</p><p><em>Yet he could say, “I know whom I have believed.”</em></p><p>Fragile faith continually asks circumstances to confirm God’s character.</p><p>Settled trust allows God’s revealed character to interpret circumstances.</p><p>That does not mean circumstances become unimportant. It means they stop being our highest authority concerning who God is.</p><p><em>Proverbs 3 says:</em></p><p>“Trust in the LORD with all thine heart; and lean not unto thine own understanding.”</p><p>The verse is sometimes treated as though God were asking us to stop thinking.</p><p>That cannot be its meaning.</p><p>Scripture repeatedly praises wisdom, knowledge, counsel and understanding.</p><p>The problem is not understanding.</p><p>The problem is leaning upon our understanding as though it were complete.</p><p>Our understanding is a gift.</p><p>It is simply not God.</p><p>Human reason can calculate what is visible.</p><p>It cannot transform partial knowledge into omniscience.</p><p>We can study.</p><p>Plan.</p><p>Ask questions.</p><p>Gather information.</p><p>Compare possibilities.</p><p>All of that can be wisdom.</p><p>But eventually every human being reaches an edge.</p><p>We arrive at a place where we have acted on everything we know and still cannot guarantee what happens next.</p><p>The believer does not meet nothing at that edge.</p><p>We meet God.</p><p>On July 20, 1969, as Apollo 11’s lunar module descended toward the Moon, computer alarms identified as 1201 and 1202 appeared. Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin did not immediately know whether the alarms required an abort. In Mission Control, specialists recognized that the guidance computer was overloaded but still completing essential tasks. Jack Garman’s preparation and Steve Bales’s judgment helped produce the “go” to continue. The landing proceeded.</p><p>The astronauts did not continue because they suddenly understood everything the specialists understood.</p><p>They continued because reliable knowledge existed beyond their immediate understanding.</p><p>That distinction helps us understand faith.</p><p>Faith does not always mean receiving an explanation.</p><p>Sometimes faith means knowing enough about the One speaking to continue without one.</p><p>This is where God’s previous acts become important.</p><p>Throughout Scripture, God repeatedly tells His people to remember.</p><p>Remember Egypt.</p><p>Remember the wilderness.</p><p>Remember His covenant.</p><p>Remember His works.</p><p>Remember His faithfulness.</p><p>Why?</p><p>Because memory supplies trust with evidence.</p><p>When present circumstances become confusing, yesterday’s revelation helps prevent us from inventing a new God today.</p><p>David faces Goliath partly by remembering the lion and the bear.</p><p>Israel’s festivals rehearse God’s acts in history.</p><p>The Lord’s Supper turns the church’s attention back to Christ’s death.</p><p>Christian faith is not built on collective amnesia.</p><p>It remembers.</p><p>And at the centre of that memory stands the cross.</p><p>Romans 8:32 keeps returning to our argument because Paul deliberately uses what God has already done to confront uncertainty about what God will do.</p><p>“He that spared not his own Son…”</p><p>The believer looks backward in order to face forward.</p><p>The cross does not tell me exactly what will happen next Tuesday.</p><p>It tells me something about the God who will still be God next Tuesday.</p><p>That may be more important.</p><p>In September 2019, India’s Chandrayaan-2 mission attempted to place the Vikram lander on the Moon. Communication was lost during the final descent, and the lander did not achieve the intended soft landing. ISRO did not treat that moment as the final definition of India’s lunar programme. Chandrayaan-3 was developed as a follow-on mission. On August 23, 2023, its lander successfully soft-landed on the Moon.</p><p>A failed attempt became information for another attempt.</p><p>That is not the same as faith in God, but it illustrates something human beings understand in other areas and often forget spiritually: one painful outcome does not contain enough information to define an entire future.</p><p>Abraham receives a promise and then waits years for Isaac.</p><p>Joseph receives dreams and then enters slavery and prison.</p><p>David is anointed king and then spends years fleeing Saul.</p><p>The disciples follow Jesus believing He is the Messiah and then watch Him crucified.</p><p>At several points in each story, visible circumstances seem to argue against what God has said.</p><p>But we read those stories differently because we know later chapters.</p><p>Joseph in prison did not yet know Genesis 50:20.</p><p>David in the wilderness had not yet experienced the throne.</p><p>The disciples on Friday had not yet seen Sunday.</p><p>We read with information they did not possess while living the moment.</p><p>That should produce humility when we read our own lives.</p><p>We do not yet have the final chapter.</p><p>God does.</p><p>This does not mean every painful event will later be explained to us neatly. Some questions may remain unanswered in this life.</p><p>Trust in God is not confidence that we will eventually receive a satisfying explanation for everything.</p><p>It is confidence that God’s character does not depend upon our access to the explanation.</p><p>This is why peace can coexist with uncertainty.</p><p>If peace requires certainty about outcomes, peace will always remain fragile.</p><p>But if peace grows from certainty about God, circumstances can move without moving our foundation with them.</p><p>Trust also produces courage.</p><p>A person convinced that everything depends upon his ability to predict the future will hesitate endlessly.</p><p>What if this fails?</p><p>What if resources run out?</p><p>What if the person says no?</p><p>What if the plan changes?</p><p>These are reasonable questions, and wisdom may require us to consider them.</p><p>But after wisdom has done its work, fear can continue asking questions forever.</p><p>At some point obedience requires movement.</p><p>Abraham had to leave.</p><p>Moses had to return to Egypt.</p><p>Israel had to walk toward the sea.</p><p>David had to approach Goliath.</p><p>Peter had to step out of the boat.</p><p>Trust does not mean they knew everything that would happen.</p><p>It means what they knew about God became sufficient for the next act of obedience.</p><p>Perhaps then the command “Trust God” is not the beginning of the argument at all.</p><p>Perhaps it is the conclusion.</p><p>We have looked at Him.</p><p>We have considered His character.</p><p>We have seen His initiative.</p><p>We have watched His dealings with weakness.</p><p>We have looked at Jesus.</p><p>We have stood before the cross.</p><p><em>And after all of that, trust begins to say:</em></p><p>I do not know everything.</p><p>But I know Him.</p><p>I cannot see the whole road.</p><p>But I know Him.</p><p>I cannot guarantee the outcome.</p><p>But I know Him.</p><p>That is what unshakable trust finally is—not certainty about everything God will do, but a settled knowledge of who God is.</p>"},{"slug":"chapter-9-living-in-the-consciousness-of-god","kicker":"Chapter Nine","title":"Living in the Consciousness of God","body":"<p>In September 2003, Nigeria launched NigeriaSat-1, its first Earth-observation satellite, as part of an international Disaster Monitoring Constellation. The project included training and technology transfer for Nigerian engineers, and the satellite later contributed imagery to disaster responses beyond Nigeria, including major international emergencies. Nigeria did not begin by possessing every space capability it might eventually desire. It entered the field through a concrete step, learned, built capacity and put what it had to work.</p><p>Large visions are often entered through available steps.</p><p>That is where this book must end.</p><p>Not with a technique.</p><p>With a way of living before God.</p><p>Biblical peace is not the condition in which nothing is wrong.</p><p>It is the condition in which what is wrong is no longer greater in our consciousness than God.</p><p>That distinction brings us to the destination of everything we have considered.</p><p>We began with grace because grace reveals God. We discovered a God who gives first, a God greater than our perspective, a God sufficient beyond our resources, a God who helps, a God who can work with what is already in our hands, and finally a God whose character becomes visible in Jesus.</p><p>The question now is what happens when that knowledge moves from doctrine into consciousness.</p><p><em>Isaiah 26:3 says:</em></p><p>“Thou wilt keep him in perfect peace, whose mind is stayed on thee: because he trusteth in thee.”</p><p>Notice where the mind is stayed.</p><p>Not on peace.</p><p>Not even on the solution.</p><p>On God.</p><p>Peace is the consequence of where consciousness rests.</p><p>This means the believer’s greatest battle in troubled times is not necessarily the battle to make trouble disappear immediately. It is often the battle over what trouble will be permitted to say about God.</p><p>A financial difficulty can become so large in consciousness that God becomes a small theological idea somewhere behind it.</p><p>A diagnosis can fill the entire field of vision.</p><p>An uncertain future can become more emotionally real than the presence of God.</p><p>A failed plan can quietly begin defining what we believe is possible.</p><p>The circumstance has not merely entered our life.</p><p>It has occupied our imagination.</p><p>Scripture repeatedly redirects the imagination toward God.</p><p><em>David says in Psalm 16, “I have set the LORD always before me.”</em></p><p>God was not literally absent until David performed a mental exercise.</p><p>David was describing intentional consciousness.</p><p>God before me.</p><p>God present.</p><p>God considered.</p><p>God allowed to become relevant to the interpretation of everything else.</p><p>That does not mean ignoring reality.</p><p>On May 29, 1953, Edmund Hillary of New Zealand and Tenzing Norgay, a Sherpa of Nepal, reached the summit of Mount Everest. Their achievement was the end of a long expedition involving route finding, multiple camps, support teams, oxygen equipment, earlier failed summit attempts and one step after another. They did not stand at the base of the mountain and act as though the summit were already beneath their feet.</p><p>They moved through the next available stage.</p><p>Large journeys are lived in available steps.</p><p>That principle becomes significant in our walk with God.</p><p>We frequently ask for resources for Step Twenty while refusing to take Step One.</p><p>We want assurance concerning the finished outcome before beginning the available obedience.</p><p><em>But courage rooted in God asks a different question:</em></p><p>What is the faithful step available now?</p><p>This does not mean every idea that comes to mind came from God.</p><p>God-consciousness does not abolish discernment.</p><p>Ideas should be examined.</p><p>Plans should be tested.</p><p>Counsel should be welcomed.</p><p>Costs should be counted.</p><p>Jesus Himself spoke about counting the cost before building.</p><p>But after wisdom has asked its legitimate questions, fear should not be permitted to impersonate wisdom indefinitely.</p><p>Fear can always discover another reason to wait.</p><p>What if I fail?</p><p>What if resources do not come?</p><p>What if people reject the idea?</p><p>What if something unexpected happens?</p><p>These questions may deserve consideration.</p><p>They do not deserve sovereignty.</p><p>At some point, the person who knows God must move with what he knows.</p><p>This is where grace protects us from both arrogance and paralysis.</p><p><em>Arrogance says, “I can do anything because I am sufficient.”</em></p><p>Grace says, “God is sufficient, so I do not need to pretend that I am.”</p><p>Paralysis says, “I cannot do everything, therefore I will do nothing.”</p><p><em>Grace says, “I can faithfully use what God has placed in my hand.”</em></p><p>Peace stands between those two errors.</p><p>It is humble enough to admit limitation and confident enough to keep moving.</p><p>On June 24, 1982, a British Airways Boeing 747 flew into volcanic ash from Mount Galunggung near Indonesia. All four engines lost power. The aircraft descended as a glider before the crew was able to restart engines after leaving the ash cloud and eventually land safely in Jakarta.</p><p>The crew did not create peace by pretending the engines were still running.</p><p>Calm was useful precisely because the emergency was real.</p><p>Biblical peace is equally capable of looking at facts.</p><p>The money may genuinely be insufficient.</p><p>The door may genuinely have closed.</p><p>The resources may genuinely be small.</p><p>The medical report may genuinely be serious.</p><p>The idea may genuinely carry risk.</p><p>Faith does not need to alter facts because God does not need protection from facts.</p><p>What faith refuses to do is allow the fact to become the whole reality.</p><p>This is where Philippians 4 becomes deeply practical. Paul tells believers not to be consumed by anxiety but to bring their requests to God with thanksgiving. Then he speaks of “the peace of God, which passeth all understanding” guarding hearts and minds.</p><p>Notice that prayer does not begin after the problem has been solved.</p><p>Peace arrives while there is still something to pray about.</p><p>We normally assume peace should follow resolution.</p><p>Problem solved, therefore peace.</p><p>Paul describes another possibility.</p><p>God known, therefore peace can begin before resolution.</p><p>This peace is not ignorance of uncertainty.</p><p>It is the refusal to face uncertainty as though God were absent from it.</p><p>And that kind of peace produces action.</p><p><em>Daniel 11:32 says:</em></p><p>“The people that do know their God shall be strong, and do exploits.”</p><p>Again, the sequence matters.</p><p>Know God.</p><p>Become strong.</p><p>Act.</p><p>The action grows out of knowledge.</p><p>This prevents Christian courage from becoming mere positive thinking.</p><p>David did not face Goliath because he had convinced himself that giants were harmless.</p><p>His argument concerned God.</p><p>Everyone else sees the same Goliath.</p><p>David introduces another reality into the conversation.</p><p>God.</p><p>The giant remains tall.</p><p>God becomes relevant.</p><p>That is God-consciousness.</p><p>It does not make the obstacle imaginary.</p><p>It refuses to discuss the obstacle as though God were imaginary.</p><p>This changes the way we respond to ideas and opportunities.</p><p>An idea may arrive before all the resources required to complete it.</p><p>Our instinct is often to compare the finished vision with our present resources.</p><p>I would need this much money.</p><p>These connections.</p><p>This equipment.</p><p>These skills.</p><p>This permission.</p><p>This team.</p><p>We compare where we are with everything the completed assignment might eventually require, and the distance between them becomes an argument for doing nothing.</p><p>But Scripture rarely gives people the entire journey in advance.</p><p>Abraham receives enough instruction to leave.</p><p>Moses receives enough to return to Egypt.</p><p>The priests approaching the Jordan have to move toward the water.</p><p>The widow receives an instruction concerning the oil she already has.</p><p>The disciples receive bread to distribute before they understand fully what Jesus is about to do.</p><p>God often gives enough for obedience without giving enough for independence.</p><p>Perhaps this is because complete independence was never the objective.</p><p>This is also why consciousness of God must become more than something we experience during prayer meetings.</p><p>God must become relevant to Monday morning.</p><p>To the office.</p><p>To the bank account.</p><p>To the proposal.</p><p>To the classroom.</p><p>To the business decision.</p><p>To the difficult conversation.</p><p>To the new idea.</p><p>To rejection.</p><p>To uncertainty.</p><p>To the moment when the resources appear embarrassingly small.</p><p><em>Hebrews 13 records God’s promise:</em></p><p>“I will never leave thee, nor forsake thee.”</p><p><em>Then the writer draws a conclusion:</em></p><p>“So that we may boldly say, The Lord is my helper.”</p><p>Presence becomes courage.</p><p><em>The believer does not say, “I have everything.”</em></p><p><em>He says, “The Lord is my helper.”</em></p><p>That is a different consciousness.</p><p>The first eight chapters of this book have not been an attempt to convince us that difficult circumstances are secretly easy.</p><p>They have been an attempt to make God larger in our knowledge.</p><p>Grace has shown us that His goodness begins in Him.</p><p>His initiative does not depend upon our deserving it.</p><p>His perspective exceeds ours.</p><p>His sufficiency exceeds our resources.</p><p>His help is not limited to the channels we expect.</p><p>He can work through what appears ordinary.</p><p>And in Jesus, we have seen His heart.</p><p>What remains is to live as though these things are true.</p><p>That may be the real test of theology.</p><p>Not whether we can describe God accurately when nothing is at stake.</p><p>But whether what we know about Him becomes available to our consciousness when everything feels at stake.</p><p>When trouble comes, can we remember who He is?</p><p>When resources shrink, can we remember who He is?</p><p>When the route changes, can we remember who He is?</p><p>When we cannot explain what is happening, can we resist creating another God from the circumstances?</p><p>When an idea is larger than our present capacity, can we begin responsibly with what is available?</p><p>The goal is not recklessness.</p><p>It is rest without paralysis.</p><p>Peace without passivity.</p><p>Courage without self-sufficiency.</p><p>Action without illusion.</p><p>The deepest resource available to the believer is not money, intelligence, opportunity, influence or even courage.</p><p>It is the knowledge of God.</p><p>Because when everything else changes, who He is does not.</p><p>So we can have peace in troubled times.</p><p>We can think while others panic.</p><p>We can pray without becoming passive.</p><p>We can use what is available without despising it.</p><p>We can receive help without worshipping the channel.</p><p>We can begin without seeing the finish.</p><p>We can admit weakness without surrendering to it.</p><p>We can move without knowing everything.</p><p>Not because we have finally become sufficient.</p><p>But because we have come to know the God of grace.</p><p>And knowing Him changes everything.</p>"},{"slug":"afterword","kicker":"Afterword","title":"Afterword","body":"<p>If there is one thing I hope remains with you after the final page of this book, it is not a technique, a formula, or even a memorable illustration.</p><p>I hope it is God.</p><p>I hope you leave these pages knowing Him more clearly than when you began.</p><p>Because eventually every circumstance changes.</p><p>Money comes and goes. Opportunities open and close. People enter and leave our lives. Plans succeed and fail. Strength rises and falls. Questions are answered, and new questions take their place.</p><p>But God remains.</p><p>That is why our deepest security cannot be built upon the stability of circumstances. Circumstances were never designed to carry that weight.</p><p>Only God can.</p><p>Perhaps you began this book facing something you could not understand. You may still not understand it.</p><p>Perhaps you began with resources that seemed insufficient. They may still appear insufficient.</p><p>Perhaps there is a prayer you hoped would have been answered by now.</p><p>Knowing God does not guarantee that every unanswered question suddenly receives an explanation.</p><p>It gives us something deeper than explanation.</p><p>Someone to trust when explanations are incomplete.</p><p>We have seen that grace begins in God. His goodness is not something we manufacture through performance. His love does not originate in our worthiness. His sufficiency does not increase when ours decreases. His wisdom does not become confused when we become confused.</p><p>We have seen that He gives first.</p><p>That His perspective exceeds ours.</p><p>That He is enough.</p><p>That He helps.</p><p>That He can work with what appears ordinary and insufficient.</p><p>And, most importantly, we have looked at Jesus.</p><p>If Christ truly reveals the Father, then we have been given something stronger than speculation about what God might be like.</p><p>We have revelation.</p><p>This means that when circumstances attempt to redefine God, we have somewhere to return.</p><p>When fear says He has abandoned us, we return to His promise.</p><p>When shame says He wants nothing to do with us, we look at Christ.</p><p>When scarcity tells us nothing can be done, we remember that what is in our hand has never been the measure of what is in God.</p><p>When uncertainty tells us we cannot move without knowing everything, we remember that faith has never required omniscience from us.</p><p>It requires trust.</p><p>And trust grows from knowing Him.</p><p>There will still be moments when you are afraid.</p><p>There will be situations in which courage does not feel automatic.</p><p>There will be days when circumstances speak very loudly.</p><p>In those moments, do not merely try harder to feel peaceful.</p><p>Return to God.</p><p><em>Ask again:</em></p><p>Who is He?</p><p>What has He revealed about Himself?</p><p>What has Jesus shown me about the Father?</p><p>What do I know about His character that this circumstance cannot change?</p><p>Then take the next faithful step.</p><p>Sometimes that step will be prayer.</p><p>Sometimes it will be work.</p><p>Sometimes it will be asking someone for help.</p><p>Sometimes it will be learning.</p><p>Sometimes it will be starting with very little.</p><p>Sometimes it will be waiting.</p><p>Sometimes it will be admitting that a plan has failed and beginning again.</p><p>But whatever the step is, take it in the consciousness of God.</p><p><em>The great invitation of grace is not merely:</em></p><p>Receive something from God.</p><p><em>It is:</em></p><p>Know God.</p><p>Because the more clearly we know Him, the less easily temporary circumstances will be allowed to define eternal realities.</p><p>And perhaps one day, after walking with Him through enough seasons, we will discover that peace has become less dependent upon what is happening around us.</p><p>Not because life became predictable.</p><p>But because God became familiar.</p><p>That is the life I hope these pages have invited you into.</p><p>A life in which grace keeps revealing God.</p><p>And knowing God keeps changing everything.</p>"},{"slug":"final-prayer","kicker":"A Final Prayer","title":"A Final Prayer","body":"<p><em>Father,</em></p><p>I want to know You as You truly are.</p><p>Not merely as my experiences have sometimes made You appear.</p><p>Not through fear.</p><p>Not through disappointment.</p><p>Not through assumptions inherited from other people.</p><p>Teach me to know You through Your Word and through Jesus Christ.</p><p>Where my understanding of You has become distorted, correct me.</p><p>Where I have imagined You reluctant, show me Your grace.</p><p>Where I have made my limitations greater than Your sufficiency, restore my perspective.</p><p>Where I have mistaken a closed channel for the loss of my Source, remind me who You are.</p><p>Where fear has kept me from using what You have already placed in my hand, give me wisdom and courage.</p><p>Where I need to wait, give me peace.</p><p>Where I need to move, give me boldness.</p><p>Where I need help, teach me to ask without shame.</p><p>Where I do not understand, keep me from making my confusion the measure of Your character.</p><p>Let my knowledge of You become greater than my consciousness of trouble.</p><p>Let Your goodness become more real to me than my fear.</p><p>Let Your presence become more influential in my decisions than uncertainty.</p><p>Let Your grace teach me that I do not have to be sufficient because You are.</p><p>And above everything else, let me know You.</p><p>Not merely know about You.</p><p>Know You.</p><p>May that knowledge produce trust.</p><p>May trust produce peace.</p><p>May peace produce courage.</p><p>And may courage produce faithful action.</p><p><em>In Jesus’ name,</em></p><p>Amen.</p>"},{"slug":"reflection","kicker":"Questions for Personal Reflection","title":"Questions for Personal Reflection","body":"<p>What picture of God have I carried most strongly through my life, and where did that picture come from?</p><p>Are there things I believe about God that are shaped more by painful experiences than by Scripture and Jesus Christ?</p><p>When circumstances become difficult, what do I instinctively assume about God?</p><p>Do I believe God is merely able to help, or do I also believe He is willing to help?</p><p>Have I confused a particular channel of provision with God Himself as my Source?</p><p>What limitation in my life have I allowed to become a conclusion about what God can do?</p><p>What is presently “in my hand”—a skill, idea, relationship, opportunity, experience, resource, or responsibility—that I may be undervaluing?</p><p>Am I waiting for complete certainty before taking a step that wisdom and obedience already make possible?</p><p>When I think about grace, do I think mainly about what God gives, or about what grace reveals concerning who God is?</p><p>What does the life of Jesus reveal about God’s attitude toward weakness, failure, suffering, outsiders, and those who need help?</p><p>What would change in my present situation if I truly lived conscious of God’s presence?</p><p>What is the next faithful step available to me now?</p>"},{"slug":"key-truths","kicker":"Key Truths to Carry With You","title":"Key Truths to Carry With You","body":"<p><strong>My perspective is real, but it is not complete.</strong></p><p><strong>“I cannot see a way” is not the same as “there is no way.”</strong></p><p><strong>God’s goodness begins in God, not in my performance.</strong></p><p><strong>Grace is not humanity persuading a reluctant God to become generous.</strong></p><p><strong>My insufficiency does not create insufficiency in God.</strong></p><p><strong>A closed channel is not the same thing as an exhausted Source.</strong></p><p><strong>God’s help may arrive in a form different from the one I expected.</strong></p><p><strong>Faith does not deny limitation; it refuses to make limitation sovereign.</strong></p><p><strong>What is in my hand does not have to explain everything God may do through my obedience.</strong></p><p><strong>Jesus is the clearest revelation of what God is like.</strong></p><p><strong>Trust is not certainty about everything God will do; it is settled confidence in who God is.</strong></p><p><strong>Peace comes from knowing who God is. Courage comes from living as though what I know about Him is true.</strong></p>"},{"slug":"scriptures","kicker":"Scriptures for Further Meditation","title":"Scriptures for Further Meditation","body":"<h2>Knowing God</h2><p>Psalm 9:10<br>Jeremiah 9:23–24<br>Daniel 11:32<br>John 17:3<br>Philippians 3:8–10</p><h2>Grace</h2><p>Exodus 34:5–7<br>John 1:14–18<br>Romans 5:6–8<br>Ephesians 2:4–10<br>2 Corinthians 12:9<br>1 Corinthians 15:10</p><h2>God’s Goodness and Love</h2><p>Psalm 34:8<br>Psalm 119:68<br>John 3:16<br>Romans 8:31–39<br>1 John 4:7–19</p><h2>God’s Sufficiency and Provision</h2><p>Psalm 23<br>2 Corinthians 9:8<br>Philippians 4:11–19<br>Matthew 6:25–34</p><h2>God Our Helper</h2><p>Psalm 46:1<br>Psalm 121<br>Isaiah 41:10–13<br>Hebrews 4:14–16<br>Hebrews 13:5–6</p><h2>Trust and Perspective</h2><p>Proverbs 3:5–6<br>Isaiah 55:8–9<br>Isaiah 26:3–4<br>2 Corinthians 4:16–18<br>Hebrews 11:1–6</p><h2>Courage and Action</h2><p>Joshua 1:5–9<br>1 Samuel 17<br>Zechariah 4:6–10<br>James 1:5–8<br>2 Timothy 1:7</p>"},{"slug":"sources","kicker":"Historical Sources and Notes","title":"Historical Sources and Notes","body":"<p>The historical and scientific events used throughout this book are illustrations rather than foundations for its theology. The theological argument is drawn from Scripture. The sources below are included to support factual verification and to preserve a clear distinction between historical illustration and biblical authority.</p><p><strong>Chapter One — Samuel Ajayi Crowther</strong></p><p>Oxford African American Studies Center, “Crowther, Samuel Ajayi”; and biographical summaries of Crowther’s capture in 1821, rescue by a British anti-slavery patrol, and consecration as bishop in 1864.</p><p>https://oxfordaasc.com/view/10.1093/acref/9780195301731.001.0001/acref-9780195301731-e-40901</p><p><strong>Chapter One — Chiune Sugihara</strong></p><p>United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Chiune (Sempo) Sugihara.”</p><p>https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/chiune-sempo-sugihara</p><p><strong>Chapter One — Abdul Sattar Edhi</strong></p><p>Edhi Foundation, “About Us,” and medical/historical profiles of Abdul Sattar Edhi’s early dispensary, epidemic response and ambulance work.</p><p>https://www.edhi.org/about-us</p><p><strong>Chapter Two — Nigeria’s 2014 Ebola outbreak</strong></p><p>World Health Organization Regional Office for Africa, “WHO Declares Nigeria Ebola-Free”; BMJ obituary of Dr. Ameyo Stella Adadevoh.</p><p>https://www.afro.who.int/countries/nigeria/news/who-declares-nigeria-ebola-free</p><p><strong>Chapter Two — Smallpox eradication in India</strong></p><p>World Health Organization, history of the global smallpox eradication programme and South-East Asia regional records.</p><p>https://www.who.int/health-topics/smallpox</p><p><strong>Chapter Two — First human heart transplant</strong></p><p>University of Cape Town, Chris Barnard Division of Cardiothoracic Surgery, “World’s First Human Heart Transplant.”</p><p>https://health.uct.ac.za/cardiothoracic-surgery/historical/first-human-heart-transplant</p><p><strong>Chapter Three — Nigeria at the 1996 Olympics</strong></p><p>FIFA, “Nigeria the first African champion | 10 great Olympic moments.”</p><p>https://www.fifa.com/en/tournaments/olympicgames/paris2024/articles/nigeria-triumph-atlanta-1996</p><p><strong>Chapter Three — Discovery of Neptune</strong></p><p>NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory, “Planetary Discovery Circumstances.”</p><p>https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/planets/discovery.html</p><p><strong>Chapter Three — Hubble Deep Field</strong></p><p>NASA Science, “Hubble’s Deep Fields.”</p><p>https://science.nasa.gov/mission/hubble/science/universe-uncovered/hubble-deep-fields/</p><p><strong>Chapter Four — M-PESA in Kenya</strong></p><p>World Bank, “M-PESA: Finding New Ways to Serve the Unbanked in Kenya.”</p><p>https://documents.worldbank.org/curated/en/775001468336854685/pdf/710710WP0Innov00Box370064B00PUBLIC0.pdf</p><p><strong>Chapter Four — Berlin Airlift</strong></p><p>Royal Air Force Museum, Berlin Airlift historical timeline and records.</p><p>https://www.rafmuseum.org.uk/research/research-enquiries/history-of-aviation-timeline/world-aviation/1949-2/</p><p><strong>Chapter Four — Chilean miners</strong></p><p>NASA History, “Chilean Miners Rescue Oral Histories.”</p><p>https://www.nasa.gov/history/history-publications-and-resources/oral-histories/chilean-miners-rescue/</p><p><strong>Chapter Five — Nigeria and wild polio</strong></p><p>WHO Regional Office for Africa, records on Nigeria’s wild-poliovirus-free status and the African Region’s 2020 certification.</p><p>https://www.afro.who.int/countries/nigeria/news/press-release-who-and-unicef-congratulate-nigeria-ending-wild-poliovirus-call-strengthening-routine</p><p><strong>Chapter Five — Dunkirk evacuation</strong></p><p>Imperial War Museums, “Dunkirk: Fact Sheet.”</p><p>https://www.iwm.org.uk/sites/default/files/press-release/dunkirk_film_2017_fact_sheet_and_iwm_resources_available.pdf</p><p><strong>Chapter Five — Tham Luang cave rescue</strong></p><p>National Geographic, reporting and documentary materials on the 2018 rescue of twelve boys and their coach in Thailand.</p><p>https://films.nationalgeographic.com/the-rescue</p><p><strong>Chapter Six — Otu Oviemo Ovadje and EAT-SET</strong></p><p>World Intellectual Property Organization, “Using IP to Attract Investors.”</p><p>https://www.wipo.int/en/web/ip-advantage/w/stories/using-ip-to-attract-investors</p><p><strong>Chapter Six — India’s Mars Orbiter Mission</strong></p><p>Indian Space Research Organisation, Mars Orbiter Mission mission book and Government of India mission records.</p><p>https://www.isro.gov.in/media_isro/pdf/Missions/PSLVC25/Illustrated_Book_on_Mars_Orbiter_Mission.pdf</p><p><strong>Chapter Six — Rosetta Stone</strong></p><p>British Museum, “The Rosetta Stone: everything you need to know.”</p><p>https://www.britishmuseum.org/blog/everything-you-ever-wanted-know-about-rosetta-stone</p><p><strong>Chapter Seven — Mary Slessor in Calabar</strong></p><p>Church of Scotland and National Library of Scotland records on Mary Slessor’s work in Calabar.</p><p>https://www.churchofscotland.org.uk/get-involved/international-work-and-global-partnerships/our-partner-churches/africa</p><p><strong>Chapter Seven — Henry Dunant and Solferino</strong></p><p>International Committee of the Red Cross, “Founding and early years of the ICRC (1863–1914).”</p><p>https://www.icrc.org/en/document/founding-and-early-years-icrc-1863-1914</p><p><strong>Chapter Seven — Baba Amte and Anandwan</strong></p><p>International Leprosy Association / WHO Goodwill Ambassador materials on Baba Amte and Anandwan.</p><p>https://leprosyhistory.org/reading-material/baba-amte-man-with-a-mission/</p><p><strong>Chapter Eight — D-Day weather forecast</strong></p><p>UK Met Office, “D-Day — the most important weather forecast in history.”</p><p>https://www.metoffice.gov.uk/about-us/who-we-are/our-history/met-office-d-day-weather-forecast</p><p><strong>Chapter Eight — Apollo 11 program alarms</strong></p><p>NASA, Apollo 11 Lunar Surface Journal and NASA history of Jack Garman’s role during the 1201/1202 alarms.</p><p>https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/apollo-era-hero-john-jack-garman-dies/</p><p><strong>Chapter Eight — Chandrayaan-2 and Chandrayaan-3</strong></p><p>Indian Space Research Organisation, Chandrayaan-2 and Chandrayaan-3 mission records.</p><p>https://www.isro.gov.in/ISRO_EN/Chandrayaan3.html</p><p><strong>Chapter Nine — NigeriaSat-1</strong></p><p>National Space Research and Development Agency (NASRDA), NigeriaSat-1 mission history; and Surrey Satellite Technology Ltd mission portfolio.</p><p>https://nasrda.gov.ng/nigersat-1/</p><p><strong>Chapter Nine — 1953 Everest ascent</strong></p><p>Royal Geographical Society, “Everest 1953.”</p><p>https://www.rgs.org/our-collections/buy-and-license-images/platinum-prints/everest-1953</p><p><strong>Chapter Nine — British Airways Flight 9</strong></p><p>International Civil Aviation Organization (ICAO), historical discussion of the 24 June 1982 volcanic-ash encounter.</p><p>https://www.icao.int/EURNAT/News%20Archives/2005/200506-Volcanic%20Ash.pdf</p>"},{"slug":"acknowledgements","kicker":"Acknowledgements","title":"Acknowledgements","body":"<p>Every book is completed within a community of influence, encouragement, conversation, correction, and grace.</p><p>I am grateful to everyone whose questions, experiences, and faith have caused me to think more deeply about the character of God.</p><p>I am especially grateful for those who have walked through difficult seasons and, sometimes without realizing it, demonstrated what it means to continue trusting God without possessing every answer.</p><p>To those who have encouraged my writing and allowed me to explore these ideas aloud before they became pages, thank you.</p><p>To teachers of Scripture whose faithful labour has helped me see the grace of God more clearly, I am grateful.</p><p>To family, friends, and the community of believers with whom I have had the privilege of sharing life and faith, thank you for the ways your lives have contributed to mine.</p><p>And ultimately, all gratitude returns to the God about whom these pages have been written.</p><p>For grace.</p><p>For patience.</p><p>For revelation.</p><p>For help.</p><p>For Christ.</p><p>And for the privilege of knowing Him.</p>"},{"slug":"about-author","kicker":"About the Author","title":"About the Author","body":"<p>Fadoju Tosin is a Christian writer with a passion for helping believers understand the grace of God and develop a practical, Scripture-centred knowledge of His character.</p><p>His writing explores the relationship between grace, faith, identity, purpose, and everyday Christian living, with particular emphasis on moving believers from performance-driven Christianity toward confidence in the character and sufficiency of God.</p><p>He is also the author of The Psychology of Grace Through Faith: Working From Grace, Not for Grace.</p><p>Through his writing and ministry, he seeks to help people move beyond merely knowing doctrines about God to developing a consciousness of God that produces trust, peace, courage, and faithful action.</p>"},{"slug":"final-word","kicker":"Final Word","title":"Final Word","body":"<p>You do not need to know everything that is ahead of you.</p><p>You need to know the One who is already there.</p><p>You do not need unlimited resources before you can take the next faithful step.</p><p>You need to know the God whose grace is sufficient.</p><p>You do not need circumstances to become perfect before you can have peace.</p><p>You need to know Him.</p><p>Know God.</p><p>Rest in His grace.</p><p>And move.</p>"}];
